"29f6ea73-9de7-55dd-ae3c-4a1047548a03" 13194139533930 true] [17592186266564 1145 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+5 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =10 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})\n$$" 13194139533930 true] [17592186266565 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266566 13194139533930 true] [17592186266565 1000 #edb/ref 17592